    "content": "Baidoa, the administrative capital of South West State, is reported to be largely calm on Thursday evening after a heavy exchange of gunfire erupted in several parts of the city and its outskirts earlier in the day. The fighting, which began around dawn, prompted residents to stay indoors as security forces engaged in operations to dislodge the attackers.\n\nThe Somali Federal Government announced that its troops successfully repelled the assault and removed the armed elements from the city. In an official statement, government and South West State officials identified the attackers as militia loyal to former South West President Abdicasis Laftagareen, and also claimed that some fighters were affiliated with the militant group Al-Shabaab. No independent verification of these allegations has been made available.\n\nThe incident marks the latest episode in a protracted political and security dispute between the federal government and Laftagareen, who was ousted last year after a contentious election. His supporters have repeatedly accused Mogadishu of marginalizing their region, while security analysts have warned that political grievances could be exploited by armed groups.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, many of whom trace their roots to Baidoa and the broader Bay region, this news serves as a reminder of the fragile security situation that persists despite years of stabilization efforts. Regular updates from diaspora networks indicate that families in the area remain wary, though no new displacements have been reported thus far. The calm, while welcome, is often tenuous in a city that has witnessed multiple attacks and political crises over the past decade.\n\nAs the night progresses, residents are hopeful that the relative peace will hold. Local authorities have called for continued vigilance and cooperation with security forces. The federal government, for its part, has reiterated its commitment to maintaining stability in the region, though questions remain about the underlying political tensions that continue to fuel sporadic violence.\n\nRead the original report from Goobjoog here: https://goobjoog.com/2026/09/03/wararkii-ugu-dambeeyay-ee-xaaladda-baydhabo/",
