    "id": "news-1788420626227-1",
    "title": "Baydhabo residents report early morning airstrikes by forces loyal to former Southwest leader",
    "original_title": "Ciidamo taabacsan Lafta-gareen oo saaka duulaan ku jarmaadiyey Magaalada Baydhabo.",
    "slug": "baydhabo-residents-report-early-morning-airstrikes-by-forces-loyal-to-former-southwest-leader",
    "summary": "Airstrikes conducted by forces aligned with former Southwest Somalia President Abdikasis Hassan Mohamed, known as Lafta-gareen, targeted areas in Baydhabo town early Thursday morning. The operation follows ongoing political tensions between Lafta-gareen and the current regional administration.",
    "content": "Residents of Baydhabo, the capital of Southwest State in Somalia, reported warplanes conducting airstrikes in the town's outskirts early on Thursday morning. The aircraft were said to be under the command of forces loyal to Abdikasis Hassan Mohamed (Lafta-gareen), the former president of the Southwest regional administration. The strikes began around dawn, sending residents into panic as they fled their homes for safety, though reports of casualties remain unconfirmed.\n\nThis operation comes amid a protracted power struggle between Lafta-gareen and the current Southwest leadership, led by President Abdiaziz Hassan Mohamed Lafta-gareen's successor, who was elected over a year ago. Lafta-gareen has repeatedly disputed the transfer of power, citing irregularities in the election process. In recent months, he has amassed troops and held meetings with clan elders and traditional leaders in the Gedo region, signaling his intent to challenge the regional government by force. The airstrikes are the latest escalation in that dispute, which has raised fears of renewed instability in an area that has seen relative calm in recent years.\n\nThe Somali federal government has not yet issued an official statement on the attacks. However, security sources suggest that the airstrikes may have been supported by international allies, possibly including African Union forces, given the complexity of such operations. Meanwhile, residents of Baydhabo, which hosts a major African Union base and has been a hub for displaced people, are concerned about the potential for further violence. \"We are worried the town will become a battlefield again,\" said one shopkeeper, who declined to be named out of fear of reprisals. \"We have seen this before, and we know what it means for our families and businesses.\"\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, particularly those from the Bay and Bakool regions, the situation is deeply concerning. Many have families in Baydhabo and rely on remittances to support them. A deterioration in security could disrupt trade routes and humanitarian access to a region already vulnerable to drought and displacement. Diaspora members have historically played a stabilizing role, funding local initiatives and pressing for peaceful dialogue, and they may again be called upon to help mediate the crisis. The international community, including the UN, has urged restraint, but with competing factions armed and ready, the path to de-escalation remains uncertain.\n\nRead the original report from Horseed Media here: https://horseedmedia.net/ciidamo-taabacsan-lafta-gareen-oo-saaka-duulaan-ku-jarmaadiyey-magaalada-baydhabo/425321/",
